  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><section id="sect-00002"><exercise id="_91b86a46-3b99-4625-ad43-24e8495050c2" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00001"><para id="para-00001">Functions \(a, b, c, d, e\), and \(f\) are given below. Which functions are linear?<emphasis>Select the three</emphasis> answers
          that apply.</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00002">Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.1. 
    </para><para id="para-00003">The correct answer is \(a(x)=3x\), \(d(x)=9+3x\), \( f(x)=9 \cdot 3x\).</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_7e5c2385-363a-4bc2-b500-8771bcfa0add" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00002"><para id="para-00004">Functions \(a, b, c, d, e\), and \(f\) are given below. Which functions are exponential?<emphasis>Select the two</emphasis> answers that apply.</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00002"><para id="para-00005">Try again. Revisit activities 5.14.1.</para><para id="para-00006">The correct answer is \(b(x)=3^x\), \(e(x)=9 \cdot 3^x\).</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="c560e6be-58d4-4a05-94bd-22d2063c7a09" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00003"><para id="para-00007">Here are 4 equations defining 4 different functions, \(a, b, c\), and \(d\). List them in order of increasing rate of change. That is, start with the one that grows the slowest and end with the one that grows the quickest. </para>
          <para id="para-00008">\(a(x)=5x+3\)</para>
            <para id="para-00009">\(b(x)=3x+5\)</para>
            <para id="para-00010">\(c(x)=x+4\)</para>
            <para id="para-00011">\(d(x)=1+4x\)</para>
       
      <para id="para-00012">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00013">Option 1: \(a(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(c(x)\), \(d(x)\)</para><para id="para-00014">Option 2: \(d(x)\), \(c(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(a(x)\)</para><para id="para-00015">Option 3: \(a(x)\),\(d(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(a(x)\)</para><para id="para-00016">Option 4: \(c(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(d(x)\), \(a(x)\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00003"><para id="para-00017">Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.3. 
    </para><para id="para-00018">The correct answer is \(c(x)\), \(b(x)\), \(d(x)\), \(a(x)\).</para></solution></exercise></section><para id="para-00019">Use the given information for problems 4 – 6. Technology is required. Function \(f\) is defined by \(f(x)=3x+5\),
  and function \(g\) is defined by \(g(x)=(1.1)^x\).</para>
<table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable" id="table-00001">
  <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis>
     </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
        \(f(x)\)
     </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
        \(g(x)\)
     </emphasis> </entry></row>
  <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        1
     </emphasis> </entry><entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        5
     </emphasis> </entry><entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        10
     </emphasis> </entry><entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        20
     </emphasis> </entry><entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>
<section id="sect-00003"><exercise id="_04eb6bed-b602-46eb-95d3-8bcd36a15239" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00004"><para id="para-00020">Which values will complete the table for \(f(x)\)?</para>
      <para id="para-00021">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00022">8, 11, 14, 17</para><para id="para-00023">8, 20, 35, 65</para><para id="para-00024">3, 15, 30, 60</para><para id="para-00025">8, 15, 30, 60</para></problem><solution id="sol-00004"><para id="para-00026">Try again. Revisit activities 5.14.2.</para><para id="para-00027">The correct answer is 8, 20, 35, 65.</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="f8c05a03-2554-4d04-9263-88fb53e061e5" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00005"><para id="para-00028">Which of the following sets of values will complete the table for \(g(x)\)?</para>
      <para id="para-00029">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00030">8, 20, 35, 65</para><para id="para-00031">1.1, 5.5, 10.10, 20.20</para><para id="para-00032">1.1, 1.6, 2.59, 6.73</para><para id="para-00033">1.1, 1.21, 1.33, 1.46</para></problem><solution id="sol-00005"><para id="para-00034">Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.2. 
    </para><para id="para-00035">The correct answer is 1.1, 1.6, 2.59, 6.73.</para></solution></exercise>
     <exercise id="_02e97ed5-02f5-4a77-affa-d83b7b8287f2" class="multiple-choice">
      <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00036">________ is a linear function.</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00006"><para id="para-00037">Try again. Revisit activities 5.14.3.</para><para id="para-00038">The correct answer is \(f(x)\) is a linear function.</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="e02206a3-611d-4186-b1af-4a9fffd02411" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00007"><para id="para-00039">________ is an exponential function.</para>
        </problem><solution id="sol-00007"><para id="para-00040">Try again. Revisit activities 5.14.3.</para><para id="para-00041">The correct answer is \(g(x)\) is an exponential function.</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_242f0919-e0cc-46f6-906d-e500e5353468" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00008"><para id="para-00042">Functions \(m\) and \(n\) are given by \(m(x)=(1.05)^x\) and \(n(x)=\frac{5}{8}x\). As \(x\) increases from
          0, __________ reaches 30 first.</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00008"><para id="para-00043">Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.3. 
    </para><para id="para-00044">The correct answer is \(n(x)\).</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="a529212b-5978-4fb5-89a6-8c64dc239be0" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00009"><para id="para-00045">Functions \(m\) and \(n\) are given by \(m(x)=(1.05)^x\) and \(n(x)=\frac{5}{8}x\). As \(x\)<emphasis>
         </emphasis> increases from 0, __________ reaches 100 first.</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00009"><para id="para-00046">Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.3. 
    </para><para id="para-00047">The correct answer is \(m(x)\).</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_274bbeb1-10b8-4f67-8aba-fcc075b326c9" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00010"><para id="para-00048">The functions \(f\) and \(g\) are defined by \(f(x)=8x+33\) and \(g(x)=2 \cdot(1.2)^x\). Which function
          eventually grows faster, \(f\) or \(g\)? Explain how you know.</para>
      <para id="para-00049">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00050">\(f(x)\) grows faster because 8 is greater than 1.2.</para><para id="para-00051">\(f(x)\) grows faster because 33 is greater than 2.</para><para id="para-00052">\(g(x)\) grows faster because it is a linear function.</para><para id="para-00053">\(g(x)\) grows faster because it is an exponential function.</para></problem><solution id="sol-00010"><para id="para-00054">Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.4. 
    </para><para id="para-00055">The correct answer is \(g(x)\) grows faster because it is an exponential function.</para></solution></exercise>
      
      <exercise id="_479162ef-4347-4bc2-aa36-7089c8ada675" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00011"><para id="para-00056">A line segment of length \(l\) is scaled by a factor of 1.5 to produce a segment with length \(m\). The new
          segment is then scaled by a factor of 1.5 to give a segment of length \(n\).
          
          What scale factor takes the segment of length \(l\) to the segment of length \(n\)? 
      </para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00011"><para id="para-00057">Try again. Revisit activities 5.14.1.</para><para id="para-00058">The correct answer is 2.25.</para></solution></exercise></section></section>
